WEBVTT

00:00:00.123 --> 00:00:02.467
{happy}Hello, and welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:04.419
If you're new here, welcome.

00:00:04.419 --> 00:00:08.455
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us.

00:00:08.455 --> 00:00:13.704
{happy}In February, India and the United States announced a trade deal.

00:00:13.704 --> 00:00:16.810
More than nine months later, it still isn't signed.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:22.252
So what duty do Indian goods face in America right now, and what is the deal still

00:00:22.252 --> 00:00:22.953
waiting for?

00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:25.355
{happy}Hi, I'm Mittal Shah.

00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:27.083
Let's walk through it, step by step.

00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:28.635
{fluent}First, what was announced.

00:00:28.635 --> 00:00:33.099
On the sixth of February, the two governments issued a joint statement.

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:38.094
The United States said it would cut its tariff on Indian goods to eighteen per cent.

00:00:38.094 --> 00:00:43.363
A tariff, also called a duty, is simply a tax a country charges on imported goods.

00:00:43.363 --> 00:00:50.010
In return, India said it intended to buy five hundred billion dollars of American

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.221
goods over five years.

00:00:51.221 --> 00:00:56.063
It also said it would lower its own duties on many American industrial and farm

00:00:56.063 --> 00:00:56.726
products.

00:00:57.154 --> 00:00:58.193
Now, here is the twist.

00:00:58.193 --> 00:01:02.928
As far as we could find, that eighteen per cent was never put in place.

00:01:02.928 --> 00:01:09.526
Two weeks later, on the twentieth of February, the United States Supreme Court ruled

00:01:09.526 --> 00:01:10.371
by six votes to three.

00:01:10.371 --> 00:01:16.254
It said the emergency law behind these tariffs does not allow a president to impose

00:01:16.254 --> 00:01:16.854
them.

00:01:16.782 --> 00:01:18.715
So what do Indian goods face today?

00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:25.338
After the ruling, the United States used a different law to charge a temporary ten

00:01:25.338 --> 00:01:26.322
per cent on imports.

00:01:26.322 --> 00:01:28.684
That ran for about five months and then ended.

00:01:28.684 --> 00:01:33.154
On the twenty-fourth of July, a new ten per cent duty began.

00:01:33.154 --> 00:01:39.245
It sits under a trade law called Section three-oh-one, after an investigation into

00:01:39.245 --> 00:01:40.225
forced labour.

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:44.393
The United States had proposed twelve and a half per cent.

00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:45.212
India got ten.

00:01:45.578 --> 00:01:47.889
Now, here is the detail that matters:

00:01:47.889 --> 00:01:51.159
that ten per cent is added on top of the normal duty.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:56.914
For example, a shirt that carried a five per cent duty would now face fifteen per

00:01:56.914 --> 00:01:57.902
cent in total.

00:01:57.902 --> 00:02:00.155
But it does not hit everything.

00:02:00.155 --> 00:02:05.310
Generic medicines and smartphones are exempt, and the Indian government says roughly

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:11.797
forty-five per cent of India's exports to the United States sit outside the new

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:12.397
duty.

00:02:12.088 --> 00:02:16.200
So the new duty is an extra ten per cent, with exemptions.

00:02:16.537 --> 00:02:17.957
As that shows, India is not the worst off.

00:02:17.957 --> 00:02:19.666
So why does it still want a deal?

00:02:19.666 --> 00:02:25.427
Because it wants a lower duty than rivals who have no deal.

00:02:25.427 --> 00:02:30.957
Indian government sources say the United States has assured India of that advantage,

00:02:30.957 --> 00:02:36.958
but only after a second Section three-oh-one investigation, into factory

00:02:36.958 --> 00:02:37.910
overcapacity, is finished.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:41.454
That assurance is reported by the Indian side.

00:02:41.454 --> 00:02:44.762
We have not seen the United States confirm it.

00:02:45.273 --> 00:02:47.861
Now, what is holding things up?

00:02:47.861 --> 00:02:52.926
Reports say the two sides still differ on tariffs, and on access to India's farm and

00:02:52.926 --> 00:02:53.834
dairy markets.

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:59.295
A two-member American team visited New Delhi on the second and third of November, to

00:02:59.295 --> 00:03:01.371
work on customs and technical details.

00:03:01.371 --> 00:03:05.367
India's commerce secretary says the talks are on the right path.

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:09.745
But as far as we could find, no legal text has been published.

00:03:09.745 --> 00:03:15.151
India says it hopes to close the first part, covering goods only, by the end of

00:03:15.151 --> 00:03:15.869
March next year.

00:03:15.869 --> 00:03:18.930
That is India's aim, not a fixed date.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.212
Finally, what don't we know?

00:03:21.212 --> 00:03:25.485
We don't know when the next formal round is, or what the first part will contain.

00:03:25.485 --> 00:03:31.393
Prime Minister Modi is in Manila this week, and President Trump is at the ASEAN-US

00:03:31.393 --> 00:03:32.327
summit there.

00:03:32.327 --> 00:03:37.018
The Indian foreign ministry said earlier this month that a meeting between them is

00:03:37.018 --> 00:03:37.912
still in the works.

00:03:38.353 --> 00:03:39.298
To sum up:

00:03:39.298 --> 00:03:42.920
what was announced in February is not what applies today.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.637
What applies is a ten per cent extra duty and a court ruling that changed the rules.

00:03:47.637 --> 00:03:52.826
Indian government sources also report an assurance of better terms, which the United

00:03:52.826 --> 00:03:55.671
States has not confirmed.

00:03:55.671 --> 00:04:00.118
The open question is what India will pay compared with its rivals.

00:04:00.118 --> 00:04:03.130
{happy}Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

